# Chapter 6, fifth part: fonts, hyphenation and what printing needs.
# Included by the chapter file.

.h2 Fonts [print-fonts]
.ix Fonts, printed
.ix Typeface, printed
.ix Latin Modern
.ix Font families
.ix Typewriter, sans serif, roman
The printed page is set in Latin Modern, the improved Computer Modern fonts
of TeX.  Three families of it are used: the roman
(serif) face for text, the sans serif face and the typewriter face.  Which
one a piece of text has depends on the font number that applies to it and on
the {b:name} of that font in the Typeface list of the Global menu (the
20 fonts of the document).  Text is set in 11 points, and in 14 points on an
envelope ({n:format-set-typefaces}); a document can name another size of 8, 9,
10, 11, 12, 14, 17 or 20 points.  Headlines are larger and notes smaller.

.h3 Which font applies
The font number of a block comes from the first of these that has one.
.table 5 55
Step | The source
1 | The font of the column (a table cell).
2 | The font of the block.
3 | The font of the named format that the block uses.
4 | The font that the level gives to headlines (Headline Font) or to text
 (Text Font), for the level that holds the block.
5 | The Headline Font or the Text Font of the Global Format settings.
.end
A Font marker changes the family from where it stands to the end of the
block.  A Keycap marker has a font number of its own.  A font number that has
no name, or is out of 1 to 20, gives the roman face.

.h3 From the name of a font to a family
The Printer reads the name of the font in the Typeface list, in lower case,
and chooses the family from the words in it.
.table 13 47
Family | Words in the name
Typewriter | program, code, courier, mono, typewriter, fixed, listing, source.
 These are looked for first.
Sans serif | head, title, sans, helv, arial, gothic.  A name that holds
 "header" (such as Header/Footer) does not count as a headline name.
Roman | Any other name.
.end
With the names that a new document has, Intro head, Level 1 head and
Level 2 head (fonts 1, 4 and 5) are sans serif, Text and Header/Footer (2 and
3) are roman, and the fonts called Font 6 and up are roman.  A font called
Program Listing would be typewriter.  To change what a font prints as,
change its name in the Typeface list.

.h3 Hyphenation [print-hyphenation]
.ix Hyphenation, printed
.ix Line breaking
.ix Even justification
.ix Soft hyphen, printed
.ix Hyphenation list, printed
The lines of a paragraph are broken as a whole, and words are hyphenated
with English patterns, whatever the language of the document.  Lines are
never left overfull when a way of breaking them exists.  Text that the block
asks to have left, right or centered is not justified; text of even
justification is.
Where the patterns break a word wrongly, say where it may be broken.  A soft hyphen
typed in a word ({n:edit-shy}) marks the places in that word; the word is then
broken only there, and only if the line ends there.  Your hyphenation list
({n:str-spell-words-hyphen}) does the same for a word in every document, and a
word in the list with no hyphen is never broken.  Both work in the preview
as in the print.

.h2 What printing needs [print-prereq]
.ix Prerequisites, printing
.ix pdflatex
.ix cups-client
Printing and previewing need programs of the system: a TeX system for the
Printer, the {c:lp} command and a queue to send a PDF to a printer, and an X
display for the Viewer.  {x:prereqs} lists what each feature needs.  None of them is
needed to edit: without them the commands of this chapter say what is
missing and the Editor goes on.
.table 26 34
Without | What you see
{c:pdflatex} | A Draft or a Final stops with "Print needs LaTeX: pdflatex was not
 found in" and the system directories.
{c:lp} | The PDF is made and the message shows that it was not sent.
An X display or the Viewer | A Preview reports this ({n:print-preview}).
.end
The PDF needs the Printer and a TeX system, not CUPS.
.note Printing never runs a program that the document names: the names of
  queues and of files are checked before they are used, and the commands that
  read or write files or run programs are refused in an equation.
